WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.090
I think it's also useful
to focus at times.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.310
It can be a focus for five days.

00:00:05.315 --> 00:00:07.170
It can be a focus for two weeks.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:12.090
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here and
today I wanna talk about focus.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.930
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:15.935 --> 00:00:17.670
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:21.400
Uh, if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:22.110
I do appreciate it.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:26.400
So my last video I spoke of
the importance of variety.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:28.620
The brain likes new things.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.110
I've often mentioned, you know,
the brain likes repetition,

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.270
but the brain likes novelty.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:38.430
And so I talked about varying the nature
of your activity, varying, you know,

00:00:38.435 --> 00:00:41.880
the kind of content you're dealing with
in terms of subject matter, in terms

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.519
of difficulty level variety is good.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.440
However, it is also
important to focus at times.

00:00:50.115 --> 00:00:55.365
Uh, so right now for the last, I would
say four or five days, I've been focusing

00:00:55.394 --> 00:01:03.465
on this tremendous content that my tutor
Sahra from Iran has sent me for Persian.

00:01:04.065 --> 00:01:09.995
It's a podcast called epitomebooks.ir.

00:01:10.634 --> 00:01:18.585
Uh, it's in Persian, of course, and he
has a five part series each one 12 to 15

00:01:18.585 --> 00:01:28.825
minutes long about the Iranian Revolution,
the Islamic Revolution ... what is it?

00:01:28.845 --> 00:01:33.375
I can't remember what he calls
it, but so for the last...

00:01:33.375 --> 00:01:37.695
and, and what I do is this, and, and it's
been very, very effective in terms of

00:01:37.785 --> 00:01:40.155
upgrading my Iranian and, or my Persian.

00:01:40.425 --> 00:01:44.390
And tomorrow I have a, a lesson with
Sahra and we're gonna talk about

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:51.929
uh, the, uh, 1979 Islamic Revolution
in Iran based on all the reading

00:01:51.929 --> 00:01:53.220
and listening that I've been doing.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:57.419
And so, so I am focusing,
but I'm not gonna focus...

00:01:57.425 --> 00:01:59.550
it's not like you have a
textbook at school and you only

00:01:59.554 --> 00:02:01.020
ever read the one textbook.

00:02:01.259 --> 00:02:04.830
I'm doing this for a week or two, and
then I'll go on to something else.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:09.750
But I just thought I would review
with you for those of you who are

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.030
interested, how I go, go about doing this.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:19.394
So I study in sentence mode because it is
just easier for me to digest the Persian

00:02:19.394 --> 00:02:21.375
language written in the Arabic script.

00:02:21.494 --> 00:02:26.834
If I go one sentence at a time,
unfortunately, with this podcast,

00:02:27.375 --> 00:02:29.625
uh, it's not timestamped.

00:02:29.655 --> 00:02:33.614
So I mean, ideal scenario is it's
timestamped, so I can hear the

00:02:33.614 --> 00:02:36.690
natural voice and see the sentence.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.750
It is the case for a lot of the
content that I get, especially

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:43.680
from YouTube, but with this
content, it's simply not the case.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:50.140
Uh, another disadvantage is that at
LingQ, you know, the, the text-to-speech

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.780
service that we use doesn't have
very high quality Fari or Persian

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.880
text to speech, but it is there.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:57.720
It's not great.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:01.140
It's, it's the worst of any of
the languages for whatever reason.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:05.399
So those are the disadvantages,
but the advantages are that I

00:03:05.399 --> 00:03:10.380
deal with this one sentence, which
might be 8, 10, 12, 15 words long.

00:03:11.459 --> 00:03:16.109
Uh, and then I you know, I, I click
on the words that I don't know, or

00:03:16.109 --> 00:03:19.530
the words that are still yellow, that
I'm still, still trying to learn.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:25.530
I review them using matching pairs,
which is the best type of review

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:30.540
because it's more forgiving, you
know, you see three different words,

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:32.730
Persian with the corresponding English.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:33.510
You gotta match them.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:34.950
If it's wrong, it goes red.

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.210
Otherwise it goes blue.

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:38.130
Get through them very quickly.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:43.050
And then I'm testing out a new
activity that we have where that

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:47.119
sentence then uh, is scrambled for me.

00:03:47.179 --> 00:03:52.250
So now I have to try to put it together
again in the right order, uh, which again,

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:54.320
allows me to focus in on this sentence.

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:58.399
So I'm focusing on the sentence
when I read it, I'm focusing

00:03:58.459 --> 00:04:00.230
on the words as I saved them.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:02.570
And I see a list of these
words that I've saved.

00:04:03.019 --> 00:04:06.620
I can go through them in other
activities, but I prefer the matching

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:09.049
pairs becasue it's easy and fast.

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:13.250
And then I try to put this
sentence back together.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.590
Now I do sometimes vary the order.

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:22.290
In other words, in order not to disrupt
the flow, uh, I'll go through, let's say

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:28.110
one episode, which say 12, 13 minutes
long and I won't do the reassembling of

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:30.300
the sentences because that takes longer.

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:35.594
So I will go through reading, looking
up words, get through the whole

00:04:35.594 --> 00:04:41.205
thing and then circle back later
on to reassemble the sentences.

00:04:41.235 --> 00:04:44.775
But the, the assembling the
sentences really gets me into the,

00:04:45.075 --> 00:04:46.844
the words and how they're used.

00:04:47.625 --> 00:04:51.195
But of course, I've also
been working around the house

00:04:51.195 --> 00:04:52.545
these last couple of days.

00:04:52.965 --> 00:04:55.785
There's been an amateur golf
tournament, the Canadian amateur

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:57.945
golf tournament at our golf club.

00:04:57.945 --> 00:05:00.645
So I haven't been able
to, uh, play any golf.

00:05:01.005 --> 00:05:04.965
So I have more time to either
work around the house or study.

00:05:05.025 --> 00:05:07.965
And of course, all the time working
around the house, I'm listening.

00:05:08.175 --> 00:05:14.445
So I'm listening to the same material,
the Iranian or the, yeah, the revolution.

00:05:14.445 --> 00:05:21.255
So I'm actually learning about the
Islamic revolution and the different

00:05:21.255 --> 00:05:28.455
factions and the ... or the landowners
or the labor group, or the ... the

00:05:28.555 --> 00:05:33.405
Muslim extremists, or the leftists
and all the different factions in

00:05:33.405 --> 00:05:40.815
groups and how they participated in or
contributed to the revolution of 1979.

00:05:41.895 --> 00:05:45.465
Part of the confusion I'm also
getting used to the Iranian years.

00:05:45.645 --> 00:05:51.315
I figured out it's 621 years
less than our Western calendar.

00:05:51.315 --> 00:05:52.485
So you get used to that.

00:05:53.025 --> 00:05:57.165
So a certain amount of focus here
has enabled me to pick up a number

00:05:57.170 --> 00:06:02.085
of political terms yet, a little
more familiar with the years that

00:06:02.085 --> 00:06:06.675
the Iranians use the month is still
a bit of a, uh, stretch for me.

00:06:07.245 --> 00:06:13.080
Um, And I get this, I feel that this
degree of focus and concentration

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:18.719
has elevated my Persian in a way,
uh, that it wouldn't, it wouldn't

00:06:18.719 --> 00:06:21.210
have been possible without this
degree of concentration and focus.

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:27.000
Now, tomorrow I will have my lesson
with my tutor and we will talk about

00:06:27.539 --> 00:06:30.480
these events and we'll see how I do.

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:35.020
Um, my hope is that my tutor
will say, wow, you've improved.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:38.055
Maybe she won't, maybe I'll be worse.

00:06:38.205 --> 00:06:42.315
I don't know, but I just thought
I would share with you that having

00:06:42.315 --> 00:06:47.685
talked about variety, that I think
it's also useful to focus at times.

00:06:48.165 --> 00:06:49.965
It can be a focus for five days.

00:06:49.965 --> 00:06:57.165
It can be a focus for two weeks, uh,
and to stay with one, um, content

00:06:57.165 --> 00:07:00.885
item, but maybe vary the way you do it.

00:07:00.885 --> 00:07:03.285
So, you know, I'm in sentence mode.

00:07:03.675 --> 00:07:07.515
Sometimes I do the, you know, putting the
sentence together after each sentence.

00:07:07.515 --> 00:07:09.945
Sometimes I save it up to do it later on.

00:07:10.155 --> 00:07:11.115
I listen to it.

00:07:11.205 --> 00:07:13.995
Another thing I did was I
printed them out on paper.

00:07:14.295 --> 00:07:17.925
And so I read them on paper, which
is again, introducing some variety

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:23.895
because if I read it on paper, I'm
now unable to look up the word, uh,

00:07:23.895 --> 00:07:28.200
rather than doing sentence by sentence
I'm now doing the whole story.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:32.850
So that's introducing some
variety while I remain focused on

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:35.490
this bit of, you know, content.

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:39.780
I should add as well that, uh,
Sahra is an outstanding tutor.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:43.680
Uh, she doesn't spend a lot of
time explaining the language to me.

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:46.020
She finds me interesting content.

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:47.310
She encourages me.

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:53.820
Uh, she has just been an unbelievable
guide to Iranian culture, food

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:56.940
history, uh, language learning content.

00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:01.140
And as I learn things of interest
to me, I don't really notice the

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:03.450
fact that I'm learning the language.

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:05.340
Although, in fact, I am
learning the language.

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:09.900
So I just thought I would share that
with you my experience over the last

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:14.510
few days, which I intend to continue
until I really feel that I've got a

00:08:14.515 --> 00:08:20.430
good grasp on this, uh, particular
series, uh, you know, and I'll leave a

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:23.790
link to it, uh, in the description box.

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:30.810
And, uh, I mean, it is part and parcel of
this whole idea of input based learning.

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:35.340
So I'll leave a couple of videos as well
that that touch on related subjects.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:39.900
Because even though what I say sometimes
seems to contradict what I said earlier,

00:08:39.905 --> 00:08:45.060
in fact, it's all part of more or
less the same message, uh, which I

00:08:45.064 --> 00:08:50.030
try to present in different ways to
see if I can stimulate people to have

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:53.700
more interest and more confidence,
uh, in their language learning.

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:54.930
Thanks for listening.

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:55.470
Bye for now.

